  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Integration Feasibility**\n\nI\'m now focusing on assessing the practicality of directly integrating saved content into a mobile keyboard. I\'m taking the user\'s non-technical constraint into account, which is shaping how I approach the answer. Specifically, I\'m now looking at available keyboard APIs and potential limitations.\n\n\n**Analyzing Technical Approaches**\n\nI\'ve determined that a custom keyboard is impossible within the existing Next.js PWA context, as web APIs lack access to IME functionality, which is required for custom keyboards. The best web approach is still clipboard storage for the user, which is already functional. However, a native application or a hybrid solution is necessary for true custom keyboard integration. I must now focus on the tradeoffs of native vs. hybrid app development.\n\n\n**Clarifying Limitations & Solutions**\n\nI\'ve clarified the limitations of the current web app setup: directly integrating a custom keyboard is impossible. I\'ve pinpointed that web apps are sandboxed, preventing access to OS keyboard APIs. To achieve this functionality, native app development is required, with specific implementations for iOS and Android. For the immediate future, I\'ll mention workarounds like utilizing the existing copy button and system clipboard features.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "That is a fantastic idea and is exactly how premium snippet apps work, but unfortunately, it is **not possible directly from a Web application or PWA**. \n\nHere is a quick breakdown of why and how it would be done:\n\n*   **Why it doesn\'t work for PWAs:** Web browsers operate inside a secure \"sandbox\" to protect user security. They do not give websites or web apps permission to access operating system-level features like the native keyboard or clipboard history monitoring.\n*   **How to actually achieve it:** To create a custom keyboard that a user can switch to in other apps (like iMessage, Instagram, or email), the app must be built as a **Native Mobile Application** rather than a web app. \n    *   **On iOS (Apple):** You would have to build a native app using Swift and create a \"Custom Keyboard Extension\".\n    *   **On Android:** You would build a native app using Kotlin and implement an \"InputMethodService\".\n*   **The web workaround:** For a PWA, the fastest workflow we can offer is what you currently have\342\200\224a one-tap \"Copy\" button. Users keep the PWA on their home screen, open it quickly, tap copy, and paste it into their chat. Users can also manually save their most-used snippets from the app into the built-in iOS \"Text Replacement\" shortcuts or the Android \"Pinned Clipboard\".\n\nWould you like to continue building out any other features for this web version?"
