  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Sharing Options**\n\nI\'ve been exploring different ways to share these snippets stored in `localStorage`. I\'ve come up with a few solutions. Specifically, I\'m leaning toward sharing snippets via URL or exporting them as a JSON file, or through the use of QR codes. However, I\'m thinking about the category issue that arises when using QR codes.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "Yes, you\'re exactly right! If we just send the snippet data (which says something like `category: \"Returns\"`) and the other device doesn\'t have a \"Returns\" category in its settings, it could cause the snippet to become \"orphaned\" or hidden.\n\nTo prevent this from breaking, when a user scans a QR code or opens a shared URL, we would write a smart **Import Function** that handles the mismatch automatically. Here is how we would solve it:\n\n### The \"Auto-Create\" Solution\nWhen sharing a snippet, we bundle both the snippet data **and** the specific category metadata (the name and emoji) into the QR code or link. \n\nWhen the receiving device reads it:\n1. **Check for Match:** It checks if a category with that name already exists in its local storage.\n2. **Auto-Create if Missing:** If it doesn\'t exist, the app automatically creates the category on the receiving device using the name and emoji provided in the link.\n3. **Save Snippet:** Finally, it saves the snippet into that category.\n\nThis ensures the snippet always has a valid home, and the recipient gets correctly set up without having to manually recreate your category structures. \n\nWould you like me to build out the **Export/Import via Link** feature with this \"auto-create category\" safety check included?"
